  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Yes, in principle: the Manual treats versions of a trade mark differing by city names or numbers as potentially forming a valid series—a ‘set’ in the terms of your question.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part13/6/6/4",
          "quote": "Example 2 IDAK IDAK SYDNEY IDAK SYDNEY 1 IDAK $500 This example is a valid series because the trade marks differ by the presence or absence of statements as to place name and number."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The versions must nevertheless resemble each other in material particulars; changing the relative prominence of the shared trade mark can make a series invalid.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part13/7/7/1",
          "quote": "The below are examples of invalid series, because the trade marks do not resemble each other in material particulars."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part13/7/7/1",
          "quote": "The large difference in the relative size of the words in each trade mark significantly changes the prominence of the shared IDAK, as well as the overall visual impression."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "A difference consisting only of city names does not itself establish a valid series: the Manual treats SYDNEY CLOTHING and MELBOURNE CLOTHING for clothing as invalid because their shared descriptive word is not an identifying feature.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part13/7/7/1",
          "quote": "CLOTHING is not likely to be taken by consumers as an identifying feature of these trade marks. Therefore, they do not resemble each other in material particulars."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "These passages explain Manual practice but do not provide the full statutory requirements or filing procedure for a series application.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
